# XBD 8.1 Environment Variable Definition
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The C tests run the test program itself as the new process image, so no
# other program or implementation detail is needed.

assertion  XBD.8.1-environment-variable-definition.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.1
class      required
since      1988
says       A variable whose value holds every byte value from 1 to 255,
           including the equals sign, reaches a new process image intact.
test       t/001-every-byte-value.c

assertion  XBD.8.1-environment-variable-definition.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.1
class      required
since      1988
says       Variables whose names differ only in letter case stay distinct in
           a new image and through setenv() and getenv().
test       t/002-case-retained.c

assertion  XBD.8.1-environment-variable-definition.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.1
class      required
since      1988
says       A name ends at the first equals sign: values containing equals
           signs, empty values and names of letters, digits and underscores
           are kept, and the new image holds exactly the strings given.
test       t/003-name-value-split.c

assertion  XBD.8.1-environment-variable-definition.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.1
class      disputed
since      2024
says       Marking PWD, OLDPWD, OPTIND, OPTARG or LINENO readonly is
           rejected, or succeeds with the shell still modifying the
           variable, or succeeds with the modifying built-in failing.
note       disputed (Q-204): LINENO is listed with the variables that built-in
           utilities modify, but the shell itself sets it and no built-in does,
           so the third alternative holds vacuously and a shell that accepts
           readonly LINENO and then no longer updates it can be read as
           conforming; the test expects the shell to keep updating it (or to
           reject the request); a FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
test       t/004-readonly-special.sh
